{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1503343838,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyMzgzMTIwOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1424#issuecomment-323831208"},"message":"Let me digest/explore a bit about storing branch name within .gitmodules since initial reaction was -- it is a recipe for disaster...  \n\nIn #748 I need(ed) a helper for \"local\" undo's, pretty much like reflog, which seems to record also the branch information.  Those \"states\" were not envisioned to be shared with anyone (the same with  reflog), so it was totally fine to store branches information","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1503344564,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyMzgzMzg1Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1424#issuecomment-323833853"},"message":"hm, it seems that in general that is already \"official\" way git submodules support branches:\n```shell\n$\u003e git submodule add --branch buga ../ds2 ds2\nCloning into '/tmp/ds3/ds2'...\ndone.\n# there was also git commit I think\n\n$\u003e ls ds2\n123@\n\n$\u003e cat .gitmodules \n[submodule \"ds2\"]\n\tpath = ds2\n\turl = ../ds2\n\tbranch = buga\n```\nand `datalad install -r` already worked out nicely with pointer(s) to specific branches.  So indeed, probably again under-used (and thus possibly buggy) feature of git submodule's, but there is already branch specification reserved in .gitmodules.\nThe question would be though, when do we add this branch'ing information?  In the case of already crawled datasets I think in 100% of the cases it is the 'master' branch is assumed. But if we implement this handling of multiple repos in sync, I can see myself annotating submodules in .gitmodules with branches for 'incoming', etc, so when we get to organizing some really large dataset (like HCP, but with archives to be extracted) I would then use it.\nBut otherwise -- at what point should be record those branch information, for any 'save subds' (or while `save -S` within subds) whenever branch in subds is not master and not explicitly known yet to .gitmodules? smells like would need a warning and explicit option (e.g. `--record-current-branch`)?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1504598603,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNzEwMTg4Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1424#issuecomment-327101886"},"message":"It seems as if the relevant code is nicely RF'ed to be able to make the change in `interface/save.py:save_dataset()`. If we see a subdataset being listed for saving, or if the entire dataset is to be saved, we update the listed branch in .gitmodules to match the checked out branch in the submodule. That requires a small change only, and should have little impact on everything else AFAICS.\n\nRe when to update this info: I think we should do this on every save, regardless of how it is invoked. The call is to be able to recall the exact state of a tree of datasets, so we need this info. subdatasets will be checked out to their exact recorded state anyways, pointing the branch to this state is just completing the restoration. Regardless of this thought, it makes sense to give that `recall` command a switch to NOT reset the branch -- often not desirable when quickly ping-pong'ing between states for testing.\n\nI would also vote for adding branch info to .gitmodules whenever a subdataset has to be saved. This would mean that if 'master' is on record, but you checked out a new branch for the same commit, .gitmodules would NOT be updated. However, if the state is changed, the associated branch is stored with it. Two advantages: 1) we do not have to select branches at random when multiple branches point to the same state -- only one branch can be checked out in one worktree at any given time. 2) we avoid cross repository comparison of properties -- avoids substantial additional complexity in decision making.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"f7ee2a4a03084a58d1d6d029a36a183aa3510117"},"timestamp":1559991174,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUwMDExNTAwMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1424#issuecomment-500115002"},"message":"Was hoping to move this along with @mih.\n\nDesired functionality:\nThis is for the afni_ci_test_data submodule of the AFNI codebase. The command\n```\ndatalad load HEAD~3\n```\n\nwould be not only checkout the parent repo commit but recursively checkout the appropriate version of submodules. \n\nOne issue that exists is that if a submodule does not exist in HEAD~3 then the tree needs to be removed and repopulated (and the submodule initialized) upon returning to the head of the branch. For this to work datalad needs to work using an alternative .git directory setup: git submodule can be setup such that all submodule .git directories are stored in the parent repo's .git. All submodule have a .git symlink that make them functional git repos.\n\nAn additional issue is considering the behavior when there are any dirty trees. Likely the command should just fail if that is the case with perhaps a force option to hard reset any offending submodules.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1559997708,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUwMDEyMTI3MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1424#issuecomment-500121270"},"message":"Debated that a little with @yarikoptic -- we concluded that we should not centralize all `.git/` directories in the parent (complicated cleanup, elevated chances for collisions).\n\nThe key problem is that we need a place to stash away subdatasets that are (momentarily) not needed for a particular state. The plan is to use a place under `.git/` of a repository to store such datasets, but limited to immediate children. Unlike git, we would use a directory that is based on a dataset's UUID. Git uses a shadow directory tree, but subdatasets could move around over time, and we would need to update the organization to reflect that. Instead we go for a flat list.\n\nThe `load()` command can then look for stashed repos, once we have recorded the datasets' UUID in `.gitmodules`.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"f7ee2a4a03084a58d1d6d029a36a183aa3510117"},"timestamp":1560008480,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUwMDEzMzcwNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1424#issuecomment-500133706"},"message":"Sounds good. Thanks.\n\nJohn\n\n\u003e On Jun 8, 2019, at 14:41, Michael Hanke \u003cnotifications@github.com\u003e wrote:\n\u003e \n\u003e Debated that a little with @yarikoptic -- we concluded that we should not centralize all .git/ directories in the parent (complicated cleanup, elevated chances for collisions).\n\u003e \n\u003e The key problem is that we need a place to stash away subdatasets that are (momentarily) not needed for a particular state. The plan is to use a place under .git/ of a repository to store such datasets, but limited to immediate children. Unlike git, we would use a directory that is based on a dataset's UUID. Git uses a shadow directory tree, but subdatasets could move around over time, and we would need to update the organization to reflect that. Instead we go for a flat list.\n\u003e \n\u003e The load() command can then look for stashed repos, once we have recorded the datasets' UUID in .gitmodules.\n\u003e \n\u003e —\n\u003e You are receiving this because you commented.\n\u003e Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub, or mute the thread.","files":null}]}